<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  The Win32 application manifest embedded in slipcase-desktop.exe, and the only
  thing in it is the DPI declaration.

  WHY IT EXISTS

  winit sets per-monitor DPI awareness at run time, inside `EventLoop::new`, and
  a measurement of the running packaged process confirms it: PER_MONITOR_AWARE.
  Two things are still worth declaring it for. The certification kit reads this
  manifest rather than the process and reported the application as not DPI aware
  because there was nothing here to read. And a declaration takes effect before
  any of this program's code runs, where the run-time call leaves a window —
  brief, possibly empty, but not nothing — in which the process exists at the
  wrong awareness.

  WHY IT IS NOT A BUILD STEP

  `DESIGN.md` §2 says nothing compiles C and a build needs a Rust toolchain and
  nothing else, and this holds to both. The usual way to embed a manifest is
  `rc.exe` or `windres`, which is what `packaging/windows/README.md` rejected
  when the window icon needed a resource — so the icon travels through
  `include_bytes!` instead. This file takes a different route: `build.rs` hands
  the MSVC linker `/MANIFEST:EMBED` and `/MANIFESTINPUT`, and the linker that
  was already linking the binary embeds it. No resource compiler, no object
  file, nothing compiled that was not compiled before.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT

  No `assemblyIdentity`. It would carry a fourth version number in a project
  whose `RELEASE.md` opens by keeping one number in three spellings, and nothing
  here reads it.

  No `<compatibility>` or `supportedOS` block. Those change what Windows reports
  to the process about itself, which is a behaviour change wearing the clothes
  of a declaration, and nothing has asked for one.

  Both values, because they are read by different vintages of Windows and must
  agree: `dpiAware` is what a pre-1703 reader understands and `dpiAwareness` is
  what everything since prefers. `PerMonitorV2` is what winit sets, and
  declaring anything else here would be a regression rather than a tidy-up.

  Author: David M. Anderson
  Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
